  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 AM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery places Upper MI in the wake of a \nsoutheast progressing trough over southern Ontario and the Lower \nGreat Lakes with a ridge building over the Midwest. Split flow \nfurther upstream features a deep trough off the coast of CA \nalongside broader troughing over the coast of British Columbia. High \npressure becomes centered over the UP and northeastern Ontario by \nsunrise, supporting calm conditions. Subsidence and low level \nmoisture under this radiative cooling setting has already yielded \nareas of fog development across the UP and out over eastern Lake \nSuperior. As temperatures settle into the mid 30s to mid 40s, \nfurther fog expansion is expected through early this morning. This \nfog could be dense at times lowering visibility down to 1/4 mile, so \ncommuters should leave extra time to reach their destination this \nmorning. Fog then slowly lifts to low clouds mid morning, lingering \ninto the late morning (early afternoon in the east/Keweenaw). Where \nclearing occurs earlier, temps warm into the 60s to low 70s while \nmid 50s to mid 60s are expected elsewhere.\n\nThe high pressure center moves east tonight to Quebec, but ridging \nremains overhead. Expect calm conditions in the south-central with \nlight southerly flow overall. This alongside onshore flow off Lake \nMichigan should support additional fog in the central and eastern UP \ntonight. Lows will be in the upper 30s to upper 40s, coolest east.\n\nThe upper level ridge becomes centered overhead on Friday as sfc high \npressure to the east strengthens. This results in an uptick in the \nsoutherly flow, warming temps into the 60s to mid 70s, warmest in \nthe west and where downslope flow assists. Partly cloudy skies are \nexpected with some CAMs suggesting isolated light rain showers \ncourtesy of the weak WAA and isentropic ascent. Kept measurable \nprecip mention out of the forecast, but on the low chance anything \ndoes resolve (5-14% chance), measurable precip beyond 0.01 \ninches and impacts are not expected.\n\nThe ridging remains overhead through most of Saturday as the western \ntroughing moves east, splitting further into a trough over the \nsouthwestern CONUS and a deeper trough over the Canadian Prairie. \nThis develops a sfc low over eastern MT on Friday which lifts \nnortheast to northern Manitoba by the end of Saturday. This keeps \nwell above normal temps (+10-25F) in the 60s to mid 70s, warmest \nwest and in downslope flow. Also expect breezy south winds gusting \nup to 20-30 mph. The low continues northeast over Hudson Bay through \nSunday as the upper level ridge flattens overhead. This sends a cold \nfront through the area late Saturday night through Sunday and brings \nback shower chances (20-40%). Significant rainfall is not expected \nat this time, probabilities of just 0.1 inches in 24 hours is only \n10-25%.\n\nHigh pressure passes east over Ontario and the Great lakes Monday \nand Tuesday favoring dry weather until late Tuesday/Wednesday when \nhigher PoPs return. That said, with colder 850mb temps moving \noverhead early next week, delta-Ts maybe sufficient for isolated \nrain showers. Troughing from the west and a resulting sfc low \npressure mid next week present better (although still low) chances \nfor precip, but ensemble guidance quickly diverges on track and \ntiming leaving low forecast confidence. The forecast reflects mostly \nslight chance PoPs (14-24%). Otherwise, temps return near normal in \nthe wake of the cold front on Sunday peaking in the mid 40s to low \n50s on Monday. Temps Monday night settle into the 20s to mid 30s, \ncoldest interior west. A slow warming trend then persists the rest \nof next week.",
